WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.669
- I hope so, I mean, I
had a great interview

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:05.138
with Colin Quinn and he
just had a book come out

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:08.942
and he was talking about how
we live in such crazy times

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:11.911 align:start size:88% position:13%
that the extremes are
telling the independents

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:12.846
that they're crazy.

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:14.314
(Rubin laughs)

00:00:14.314 --> 00:00:17.751
And he was saying, you know,
this is the majority of people,

00:00:17.751 --> 00:00:19.853
how crazy is it that people ...

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:21.388
He is like, they're the craziest

00:00:21.388 --> 00:00:22.856
they're the ideologues.

00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:25.425
(upbeat music)

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:36.336 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- It's Locals week here
on the Rubin report

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:38.538 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
and I'm doing a series
of mini interviews

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:41.374 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
with creators on why they
decided to become less reliant

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:45.078
on Big Tech and instead own
their content, community

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:47.580
and livelihood with locals.com.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.217
Joining me today is comedian
and host of the dumpster fire,

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:53.620
Bridget Phetasy, welcome
back to the Rubin report.

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:56.556
- Thank you for having
me, always a pleasure.

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:58.525
- It's nice to see you again,

00:00:58.525 --> 00:01:00.493 align:start size:94% position:6%
before we get into your..
- It's been too long,

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:01.995
- Your locals community,

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:06.399 align:start position:0%
and you were literally the first
person that I got on Locals

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:10.270
besides me, I thought
we'd talk a little bit

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:11.538
about why...

00:01:11.538 --> 00:01:12.939
I thought you'd be
the right person,

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:15.642
and it's because you are trying

00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:17.277
sorta like I'm trying, I think,

00:01:17.277 --> 00:01:20.480 align:start size:94% position:6%
to bring a little civility
back to discourse,

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.781
how's that going for you?

00:01:23.016 --> 00:01:25.018
- It's going well in the
community.(Rubin laughs)

00:01:25.018 --> 00:01:29.522 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
I mean it is, the last
debate we did a live chat,

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:33.159 line:0%
which was really fun because
that's like the best feature

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:37.730 line:0%
ever that brings everybody back
to kind of a chat room vibe,

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.265
which is amazing.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:41.367 align:start size:84% position:16%
And everybody loved
it, and we did.

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:43.603
And you know, I had
some Biden supporters

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:45.205
some completely independent

00:01:45.205 --> 00:01:46.940 align:start size:84% position:16%
some people who
just aren't voting,

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.541
people who are pro-Trump,

00:01:48.541 --> 00:01:52.011 align:start size:97% position:3%
all of us were in the chat,
everybody was laughing,

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:53.580
nobody was fighting.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.915
It was very civil, people
were having disagreements,

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:59.385
but still being cordial to
one another, it was amazing.

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:02.589 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I believe that
it's still out there.

00:02:02.589 --> 00:02:05.825
- Is it weird that you have
to sort of cultivate a space

00:02:05.825 --> 00:02:07.227
for that?

00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:08.928 align:start position:0%
Like, I think what intrinsically
comes to people like me

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:12.332
and you pretty obviously,
pretty simply naturally

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:14.801 align:start size:84% position:16%
that these days
it's just like gone.

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:19.606
- Yeah. I think it's...

00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:24.077
I feel like it's less gone in
real life than it is online.

00:02:24.077 --> 00:02:27.213
So I think if you got a
group of people together,

00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:29.816
they probably would

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:32.719 align:start size:97% position:3%
Even if they fell all across
the political spectrum,

00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:35.488 align:start size:88% position:13%
I would hope they would
manage to be civil.

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:37.257
And I imagine they
would be more civil

00:02:37.257 --> 00:02:38.992
than perhaps they
would be online

00:02:38.992 --> 00:02:43.730 align:start size:97% position:3%
although everybody being
quarantined and being online,

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:47.934 align:start position:0%
I feel like some of that
decorum has eroded in that time

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:52.238 align:start size:97% position:3%
and people are forgetting
how to behave online and off

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:53.806
completely.

00:02:53.806 --> 00:02:56.442
And they're becoming more
and more kind of polarized

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:58.511
or entrenched in
their own beliefs.

00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:03.416
So it seems like things
because of the pandemic,

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:08.821
it kind of accelerated this
already rude and nasty tone

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:12.692
that we were already
seeing amongst people.

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:17.197 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I do think, you know,
online, it's worse obviously

00:03:17.197 --> 00:03:19.432
people are much they're...

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:20.600
We are anonymous

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:23.870 align:start size:88% position:13%
I really encourage
people in the community

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.673
to use their name, to
use their picture too,

00:03:26.673 --> 00:03:29.008 align:start size:97% position:3%
so that we can get to know
each other and help each other

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:31.277
and have it feel more
like a neighborhood

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:33.580
and less like a social media

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.316
where everybody's just, you
know, outwitting each other

00:03:36.316 --> 00:03:38.384
and trying to be the snarkiest.

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:40.320
- Yeah one of things
that you push a lot

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.521
that I always appreciate

00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:43.590
is that it's not
all about politics.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.526 align:start position:0%
Like, even if you love politics,
even if you live politics

00:03:46.526 --> 00:03:47.727
and I love politics

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:49.262 align:start size:84% position:16%
and what I've been
doing for a long time,

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:53.333
but that life is just
not about only that.

00:03:54.701 --> 00:03:56.269
- Yeah, I hate politics

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:58.438
(both laugh)

00:03:58.438 --> 00:04:02.342
And falling into this space has
made me hate them even more.

00:04:02.342 --> 00:04:06.012 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it's only affirmed
everything that
I believed before

00:04:06.012 --> 00:04:07.747
I knew anything about politics,

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:09.882
which is that they're dirty

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:13.886
and they bring out kind
of the worst in people.

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:17.523
And it's not my favorite topic.

00:04:17.523 --> 00:04:19.826
And my community is very...

00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:24.230
You know, I'm sober,
so I try to talk a lot

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.966 align:start size:88% position:13%
about just what I've
learned in coping tools.

00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:29.235
I feel grateful to have
learned it in sobriety.

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:31.838
You don't have to be
sober to have access

00:04:31.838 --> 00:04:33.006
to a lot of this stuff.

00:04:33.006 --> 00:04:36.409 align:start size:84% position:16%
And my whole podcasts
Walk-ins Welcome

00:04:36.409 --> 00:04:38.111
is about grit and resilience.

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:41.047
And so I do my best to...

00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:43.916 align:start size:97% position:3%
That psychological
cultivation of that attitude

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:46.219 align:start size:84% position:16%
is really the most
important thing to me.

00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:49.889
And I think in the community,
that's the vibe of everybody

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:51.157
supporting each other.

00:04:51.157 --> 00:04:53.326
I have workouts with the
girls five days a week.

00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:55.728
So we do zoom workouts
and it's been amazing.

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:59.098 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, this community
has truly saved me

00:04:59.098 --> 00:05:01.000
throughout quarantine
and in the pandemic

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.169
just keeping me on the beam

00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:07.807
and working out and working
on ourself and sharing goals.

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:10.810
And I've seen people help
each other through death.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:14.280 align:start position:0%
I've seen people help each
other through health struggles.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.684 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's just been really
inspiring to see

00:05:17.684 --> 00:05:19.986 align:start size:84% position:16%
this virtual community
support each other

00:05:19.986 --> 00:05:22.088
the way that community should.

00:05:22.088 --> 00:05:23.623
- Yeah. Well, it's funny to me

00:05:23.623 --> 00:05:28.227
because you're right, like we
all got sort of jammed online

00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:29.862
even more because of COVID.

00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:32.865 align:start position:0%
We knew there was like a problem
with the online discourse

00:05:32.865 --> 00:05:35.968 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then the solution somehow
is that we're all online,

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:37.970
even more and doing
everything there.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:39.339
And it's like, we
need those spaces.

00:05:39.339 --> 00:05:41.774
I'm having dinners with
people in the community

00:05:41.774 --> 00:05:45.244
and we've hired several
people for Rubin Report

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:47.180
and for Locals that have
come out of the community

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:48.681
and all that.

00:05:48.681 --> 00:05:50.183
One of the things that I
think you're trying to do

00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:53.986
also is carve out a space
for people to still be,

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:55.855
still be centrist.

00:05:55.855 --> 00:05:58.291 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you think the centrist
thing can survive

00:05:58.291 --> 00:06:01.260
the crazy part is in
times that we live in?

00:06:02.462 --> 00:06:05.331
- I hope so. I mean, I
had a great interview

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:07.700
with Colin Quinn and he
just had a book come out

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:11.504
and he was talking about how
we live in such crazy times

00:06:11.504 --> 00:06:14.474 align:start size:88% position:13%
that the extremes are
telling the independents

00:06:14.474 --> 00:06:15.908 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they're crazy.
(Rubin laughs)

00:06:15.908 --> 00:06:17.176 align:start size:97% position:3%
And he was saying, you
know, "this is the majority

00:06:17.176 --> 00:06:21.948
of people, how crazy
is it that people...

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:23.916
He's like, they're the crazies,

00:06:23.916 --> 00:06:25.251
they're the ideologues,

00:06:25.251 --> 00:06:27.453
they're the ones who are extreme

00:06:27.453 --> 00:06:29.188
and will die on whatever hell

00:06:29.188 --> 00:06:32.225 align:start size:97% position:3%
and carry, you know, whatever
water they need to carry,

00:06:32.225 --> 00:06:35.962
to justify whatever they need
to justify to themselves."

00:06:35.962 --> 00:06:38.698
And I think people in
the middle, you know,

00:06:38.698 --> 00:06:41.934 align:start size:94% position:6%
more than anything, I just
want people to be okay

00:06:41.934 --> 00:06:44.170
with expressing confusion.

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:47.673
Or expressing doubt or
expressing uncertainty

00:06:47.673 --> 00:06:50.777
or expressing the I don't know,

00:06:50.777 --> 00:06:53.279
that's a perfectly
acceptable answer.

00:06:53.279 --> 00:06:57.116 align:start size:94% position:6%
And these times it is
crazy out there. And so...

00:06:57.116 --> 00:06:58.618
- Wait a minute, wait a minute.

00:06:58.618 --> 00:07:00.420
Are you saying you don't know
how much yellow cake uranium

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:02.422
(indistinct) in centrifuges is?

00:07:03.556 --> 00:07:05.057
- No, I know nothing.

00:07:05.057 --> 00:07:07.093 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Do they even put yellow
cake uranium in centrifuges?

00:07:07.093 --> 00:07:08.127
That just sounds like something.

00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:10.863
(Bridget laughs)

00:07:10.863 --> 00:07:12.899
- I know, less than nothing.

00:07:12.899 --> 00:07:16.068 align:start size:84% position:16%
The more I've entered
into this realm

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:19.038
the more I've realized, I
know it's so very little

00:07:19.038 --> 00:07:20.540
about anything.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:24.677
And it's easy to just get
online and become an expert

00:07:24.677 --> 00:07:26.279
in whatever is trending

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:28.981
because you can Wikipedia things

00:07:28.981 --> 00:07:31.150
and become this kind
of armchair expert

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:33.920 align:start size:84% position:16%
in any single
thing that pops up.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:38.758 align:start size:97% position:3%
But really, we don't know that
much unless we are experts.

00:07:38.758 --> 00:07:42.929 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I'm okay with
being kind of the dummy

00:07:45.231 --> 00:07:47.200
who is asking a
lot of questions.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:49.035
And that's the other thing
I'm noticing is that people

00:07:49.035 --> 00:07:50.770 align:start size:81% position:19%
are afraid to even
ask questions.

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:53.539
It's not even vote for X, Y, Z.

00:07:53.539 --> 00:07:55.074
If you don't say...

00:07:55.074 --> 00:07:59.045
If you say I'm not voting
or I'm voting third party,

00:07:59.045 --> 00:08:02.648 align:start size:94% position:6%
you will basically be
called a fascist as well.

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:04.517 align:start size:88% position:13%
- You are a facilitator
or something

00:08:06.519 --> 00:08:09.355
- It's fascinating
to me to just see how

00:08:10.957 --> 00:08:15.962 align:start size:94% position:6%
that there's a loss of
people being able to speak

00:08:17.096 --> 00:08:20.733
their free and honest
opinion and truth,

00:08:20.733 --> 00:08:25.738 align:start position:0%
and so more important than
anything is that we all continue

00:08:26.906 --> 00:08:29.342 line:0%
to cultivate spaces
where that's okay.

00:08:29.342 --> 00:08:31.811 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
Where people feel like
they can just say,

00:08:31.811 --> 00:08:34.113 line:0%
I mean, I can't tell you how
many emails I get from people

00:08:34.113 --> 00:08:35.748
who are politically homeless,

00:08:35.748 --> 00:08:38.084 align:start size:94% position:6%
who are like, I don't
even care if you read this

00:08:38.084 --> 00:08:40.152
that just felt so good to say.

00:08:40.152 --> 00:08:43.022 align:start position:0%
Because they don't have
anywhere they can express this.

00:08:43.022 --> 00:08:45.658 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I'm hoping that my
community can be that place

00:08:45.658 --> 00:08:50.029 align:start size:88% position:13%
where people can
express their confusion.

00:08:50.029 --> 00:08:51.931
I think Jack Kerouac
had a great quote.

00:08:51.931 --> 00:08:54.333 align:start size:97% position:3%
"I have nothing to offer the
world, but my confusion."

00:08:54.333 --> 00:08:57.403
I feel like that's
very much my vibe.

00:08:57.403 --> 00:08:58.871
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:08:58.871 --> 00:09:00.406 align:start position:0%
Well, I've jumped into your
community and I just kind of see

00:09:00.406 --> 00:09:03.543 align:start position:0%
what's going on, it's like
people are sharing other things

00:09:03.543 --> 00:09:07.513
besides politics, and they're
trying to help each other out.

00:09:07.513 --> 00:09:09.949
And it's like that
really remember...

00:09:09.949 --> 00:09:11.384 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Lotta foodies
- The word social,

00:09:11.384 --> 00:09:12.919
is in social media.

00:09:12.919 --> 00:09:14.287
It's like it was supposed
to make... Right foodies,

00:09:14.287 --> 00:09:16.522 align:start size:88% position:13%
and what do you have
for dinner and all that?

00:09:16.522 --> 00:09:19.559 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean half of my community
is just doing recipes

00:09:19.559 --> 00:09:22.228 align:start size:97% position:3%
and dinners, but it's like,
- (indistinct)

00:09:22.228 --> 00:09:23.663
- You're smiling as
you're saying it,

00:09:23.663 --> 00:09:26.365 align:start size:97% position:3%
because it's like, Oh, there's
something good happening

00:09:26.365 --> 00:09:28.034
there, but it's like,
people are on Twitter

00:09:28.034 --> 00:09:29.969 align:start size:97% position:3%
just telling you how miserable
they are all day long

00:09:29.969 --> 00:09:31.637 align:start size:97% position:3%
and yet we're there too. So_
- Yeah.

00:09:31.637 --> 00:09:32.538
- Irony, whatever.

00:09:34.273 --> 00:09:38.244 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah. I mean, I like
Twitter because I really feel

00:09:38.244 --> 00:09:40.146
like it's the Thunderdome.

00:09:40.146 --> 00:09:42.381
So you get to just...

00:09:43.716 --> 00:09:45.751
It's very hard to
be free there too.

00:09:45.751 --> 00:09:49.422 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I like to challenge
myself on Twitter

00:09:49.422 --> 00:09:53.326
because it's an exercise
in what I'm not saying.

00:09:53.326 --> 00:09:55.595
So when it first started,

00:09:55.595 --> 00:09:58.464
when I first went on Twitter
and started getting noticed

00:09:58.464 --> 00:10:02.935 align:start position:0%
even by people on the right
you'll lean into what's working.

00:10:02.935 --> 00:10:06.505
And I generally have this rule
where wherever I have a tweet

00:10:06.505 --> 00:10:09.909
that goes really big,
I'll do the opposite.

00:10:09.909 --> 00:10:13.379
Or I'll say something just so
that I can weed out anybody

00:10:13.379 --> 00:10:17.550
who might think that I'm
too much of a, you know,

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:18.818
you can see one tweet

00:10:18.818 --> 00:10:20.620 align:start size:88% position:13%
and be like, Oh, I know
who this person is,

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:24.490 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I need to weed out the
people who can't handle

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:26.025
differing opinions.

00:10:26.025 --> 00:10:29.328
Because on both sides there
are people who can't handle

00:10:29.328 --> 00:10:31.597 align:start size:84% position:16%
you saying anything
bad about Trump

00:10:31.597 --> 00:10:34.033
or anything bad about
the left.(chuckles)

00:10:34.033 --> 00:10:37.069
- Yeah. Where do you
see the internet going

00:10:37.069 --> 00:10:38.838
in the next couple of years?

00:10:38.838 --> 00:10:40.573
Do you think think
these big things

00:10:40.573 --> 00:10:42.708
that they'll exist still,

00:10:42.708 --> 00:10:44.577 align:start size:94% position:6%
it seems like everything's
kind of crumbling.

00:10:44.577 --> 00:10:45.778 align:start size:81% position:19%
Maybe they'll look
very different.

00:10:45.778 --> 00:10:48.014 align:start size:88% position:13%
I've been asking
everybody this question.

00:10:49.281 --> 00:10:51.283
- Yeah, that's a good question.

00:10:53.719 --> 00:10:57.823
I don't know where anything
will be in two years or three

00:10:57.823 --> 00:10:59.091
in the future.

00:10:59.091 --> 00:11:03.362
I feel like all of the
things that we never

00:11:03.362 --> 00:11:06.766 align:start size:88% position:13%
could have considered
possible are playing out

00:11:06.766 --> 00:11:08.200
are happening.

